                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "34b49c65-9129-3e5f-97a2-f40522ad4ad7",
                        "entry_id": "1cd0f7a7-c853-33c5-a739-8aa9f602cda7",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Mot qui ressemble à un mot d’une autre langue mais de sens différent, partiellement (faux-ami partiel) ou totalement (faux-ami absolu ou faux-ami total)."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "21940efc-827b-4c74-8b84-411450ff5ff0",
                                "sense_id": "34b49c65-9129-3e5f-97a2-f40522ad4ad7",
                                "text": "L’anglais apology est un faux-ami du français apologie et signifie « excuse »."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "18bc7207-831f-4045-bf79-d0fb552cd29a",
                                "sense_id": "34b49c65-9129-3e5f-97a2-f40522ad4ad7",
                                "text": "L’allemand fast, qui signifie « presque », est un faux-ami du mot anglais fast qui, lui, signifie « rapide »."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "046dd006-0651-47fa-927d-c085d7aa9f7c",
                        "source_entry_id": "1cd0f7a7-c853-33c5-a739-8aa9f602cda7",
                        "source_sense_id": "34b49c65-9129-3e5f-97a2-f40522ad4ad7",
                        "source_sense_description": "(Linguistique) Mot qui ressemble à un mot d’une autre langue mais de sens différent",
                        "target_lang_word": "falscher Freund",
                        "target_lang_code": "de",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "falscher Freund",
                                "target_lang_code": "de",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "3cb088d0-9769-325a-8278-97bfc69822bf",
                                        "entry_id": "27273876-f869-3ae4-9f6d-4cac0f00879a",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Wort in einem Wortpaar, das aus in zwei Sprachen in gleicher oder ähnlicher Form vorkommenden Wörtern besteht, die aber nicht dasselbe, sondern Verschiedenes bedeuten"
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "oft im Plural"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "3767f7c3-c1c7-4755-9232-aba3cad99b12",
                                                "sense_id": "3cb088d0-9769-325a-8278-97bfc69822bf",
                                                "text": "Deutsch „Gymnasium“ und englisch „gymnasium“ sind falsche Freunde: Das deutsche Wort \"Gymnasium“ hat eine gleich geschriebene Entsprechung im Englischen: „gymnasium“, das aber nicht eine Schulform meint, sondern „Turnhalle“ bedeutet."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "77cf9ae4-c1d9-40ee-8cbc-e9481346af28",
                                                "sense_id": "3cb088d0-9769-325a-8278-97bfc69822bf",
                                                "text": "Deutsch „Physiker“ und englisch „physician“ sind ebenfalls falsche Freunde: Das englische Wort „physician“ bedeutet im Deutschen „Arzt“ (nicht: „Physiker“)."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "d0236b0c-ced8-4091-9113-a46df0884df0",
                                                "sense_id": "3cb088d0-9769-325a-8278-97bfc69822bf",
                                                "text": "„Was sind nun eigentlich - sprachwissenschaftlich betrachtet - falsche Freunde oder ‚false friends‘? Es sind Wörter, bei denen in zwei oder mehreren Sprachen Schreibweise ‚und‘ Klang bzw. Schreibweise ‚oder‘ Klang eine identische oder zumindest sehr ähnliche Form aufweisen, so daß man leicht zu falschen Schlüssen hinsichtlich einer vermuteten Bedeutungsgleichheit gelangt.“"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "cd0df578-5b99-40cb-aea2-4824541d08d2",
                                                "sense_id": "3cb088d0-9769-325a-8278-97bfc69822bf",
                                                "text": "„Scheinentlehnungen und falsche Freunde gehören zu den Phänomenen, die für die negative Einstellung der Öffentlichkeit gegenüber Anglizismen verantwortlich sind.“"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "b2c9b76c-4f9a-4e42-9221-c18f53a14a8a",
                                                "sense_id": "3cb088d0-9769-325a-8278-97bfc69822bf",
                                                "text": "„Für Fremdsprachenlerner stellen vor allem Äquivalenzen auf der Bedeutungsseite eine Schwierigkeit dar, können doch falsche Freunde wie »reden wie ein Buch«, das im Spanischen nicht ›viel‹, sondern ›klug reden‹ bedeutet, unerkannt bleiben.“"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "e4697514-ba28-4fa1-b6e4-ac3012ef6524",
                                                "sense_id": "3cb088d0-9769-325a-8278-97bfc69822bf",
                                                "text": "„Insofern ist das Wort für uns und für die Franzosen, die Deutsch lernen, ein sogenannter »falscher Freund«.“"
                                            }
                                        ],
                                        "sense_index_json": "\"1\""
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "translation_id": "270e8af4-898c-4e21-b7f8-fc66b88c05d5",
                        "source_entry_id": "1cd0f7a7-c853-33c5-a739-8aa9f602cda7",
                        "source_sense_id": "34b49c65-9129-3e5f-97a2-f40522ad4ad7",
                        "source_sense_description": "(Linguistique) Mot qui ressemble à un mot d’une autre langue mais de sens différent",
                        "target_lang_word": "false friend",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "false friend",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "0d4cedfa-1e58-34f0-9313-c4cea81e06f6",
                                        "entry_id": "01af2f2b-2a77-39f9-966d-e271bf578ba1",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A word in a language that bears a deceptive resemblance to a word in another language but in fact has a different meaning."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "29f7af53-5ee9-4cc1-b436-6b1a6bc1e2fc",
                                                "sense_id": "0d4cedfa-1e58-34f0-9313-c4cea81e06f6",
                                                "text": "A word and its false friend may well be etymologically related: in such cases semantic shifts have made them drift apart."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "ebfe93fb-04ff-3d9e-8127-60d11dd6caad",
                                        "entry_id": "01af2f2b-2a77-39f9-966d-e271bf578ba1",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Used other than figuratively or idiomatically: see false, friend."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": []
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "translation_id": "d9120cac-fd14-4bb4-9dcb-bb068059fe9b",
                        "source_entry_id": "1cd0f7a7-c853-33c5-a739-8aa9f602cda7",
                        "source_sense_id": "34b49c65-9129-3e5f-97a2-f40522ad4ad7",
                        "source_sense_description": "(Linguistique) Mot qui ressemble à un mot d’une autre langue mais de sens différent",
                        "target_lang_word": "false cognate",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "false cognate",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "f69832d7-8b5f-3063-93de-934c4eece8d0",
                                        "entry_id": "996bcd64-1c50-3012-887b-64e8cd975e32",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A word that appears to be cognate (etymologically related) to another given word, but in fact is not."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "5247d658-a3a9-4bf8-b641-821be8c6efe3",
                                                "sense_id": "f69832d7-8b5f-3063-93de-934c4eece8d0",
                                                "text": "False cognates are words in different languages that are similar in form and meaning but have different roots. They appear to have a common linguistic origin (regardless of meaning) but actually do not. [...] The two terms, \"false cognates\" and \"false friends,\" are sometimes used incorrectly or interchangeably by some teachers[. ... Learn to] recognize false cognates, which are pairs of words in different languages that are similar in form and meaning but have different roots."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "d8b9f5c8-8b04-4f83-be34-4477a6c95e88",
                                                "sense_id": "f69832d7-8b5f-3063-93de-934c4eece8d0",
                                                "text": "I don't speak Chinese, but \"Mama\" is a false cognate, means the same thing in English, Swahili, Navajo--"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "dddcd8d8-b728-4f60-b700-28204d230049",
                                                "sense_id": "f69832d7-8b5f-3063-93de-934c4eece8d0",
                                                "text": "Conversely, the Italian word cazzo [cock, penis] and the Spanish word cazo [ladle, small saucepan] would be false friends and false cognates inasmuch as their respective meanings are different; additionally, there is not any etymological [...] common root for both words. This makes the set of false friends wider than the set of false cognates, since all false cognates are false friends, but not all false friends are false cognates."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "8af88a43-21bd-3b2e-8922-ca1ee8bac350",
                                        "entry_id": "996bcd64-1c50-3012-887b-64e8cd975e32",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A false friend: a word that appears to have the same meaning as another given word, but in fact does not (without regard to whether or not the two terms are cognate)."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "proscribed"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "a723b71e-384f-4f12-8b99-952792e98a6d",
                                                "sense_id": "8af88a43-21bd-3b2e-8922-ca1ee8bac350",
                                                "text": "False cognates, on the other hand, are words in two languages that are identical or similar in form and may mislead the bilingual to think that they have the same or similar meaning, while their meaning is actually different, e.g., become-bekommen in English-German, […]"
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
